{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563102-i-ve-come-to-really-appreciate-the-storyteller-v20-or-earlier-wound-system","id":1563102,"title":"I've come to really appreciate the storyteller(V20 or earlier) wound system","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-08-14T11:04:15-07:00","id":1563102,"title":"I've come to really appreciate the storyteller(V20 or earlier) wound system","content":"Forcing players to spend blood to heal is just a really elegant way to force some partial loss state into the system.<br /> <br /> They take some lethal, blood all over their clothes, open wound, masquerade under threat, must either spend more blood than they want and risk hunger frenzy or hide.<br /> <br /> You can easily key it into extra in in universe complications; someone shot you in the face, your jaw hangs open you can barely speak, axe to the leg your leg is broken you can't walk right, burns, everything is just pain. If you want some inconvenient healing just hand out some aggravated.<br /> <br /> I like dnd to but hp does nothing until you hit 0.<br /> And games were normal humans get 'wounds' require either external magic healing or it gets too inconvenient to heal. And its a bit clunkier because they only get wounds it doesn't actually affect any core resource that powers all their other cool stuff.<br /> <br /> Though I've heard Kult<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />ivinity lost should have a decent one but I don't know it. <br /> <br /> If you want lots of action scenes you need a supernatural way to heal, or lots of backup characters. Or some action movie logic to healing. Vtm just has it baked in.<br /> <br /> Plus going to 0 isn't either an instant death or 'just some healing and you're up again' you're officially knocked out for weeks or months unless you're a very particular vampire. It's a nice middle ground between 'lost forever' and 'trivial stability check/healing potion'<br /> <br /> I've just come to appreciate it, even if there's few ways to be very resilient and it breaks apart at high dice pools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-14T23:00:11-07:00","id":1563114,"title":"","content":"I have conflicting feelings about it.<br /> <br /> For one, I don't think wound penalties add realism. That implies damage is literal, physical wounds, and modeling actual wounds is far too complex for a TTRPG. The penalties only hold as long as you don't ask what exactly was hit.<br /> <br /> And it isn't fun, either. The penalties stack so fast that any wound that lasts enough to apply them makes it very hard to keep playing. If it is to have this effect, I'd rather have the new V6 Playtest &quot;Health = Vitae&quot; system.<br /> <br /> D&amp;D isn't perfect, but it gives one important option: to treat HP as luck. You never got hit in the first place. As an action hero you can push through a lot of stuff, but you also just get to brush with death and get away with it time and again. Part fate, part the luck of the daring, and part confidence that you can still push further.<br /> <br /> Mutants and Masterminds has an interesting system with the resistance roll. Damage is a DC, you roll a save against it, if you fail the margin tells what happens, with the basic effect being cumulative penalties. It captures how real wounds work much better in that even if your actual chances are too good, the sense of regular mortality is there.<br /> <br /> Being a vampire helps, but seriously, I don't feel like being superior to mortals because you have the advantage of being a functional character is the best way to contrast things.<br /> <br /> What does make me grudgingly accept it isn't the contrast or any Masquerade worries. It's the very notion that combat is highly lethal. I came to appreciate to a degree the tactical constraint of a system where no damage can be brushed off, where you are pressed to take care of your safety first or things can go bad really fast.<br /> <br /> That's not something I want in every game, though, or the best way to convey this theme.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-15T02:21:43-07:00","id":1563118,"title":"","content":"Personally, I like how classic WoD damage is based on narrative health levels. I always treated the penalties as the impact of pain, distraction, loss of morale, etc., but you can, in theory, also abstract them to cover specific injuries\u2014e.g., \u20135 might be that you've lost your dominant arm. The death spiral is real, but frenzy is always a risk at that point anyway, and that negates wound penalties.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-15T04:02:14-07:00","id":1563120,"title":"","content":"For Vampire I like to play up physical wounds because it's part of the genre. There's always that scene where a vampire have some gruesome wound and then it slowly starts to knit itself together and it stands up, fangs and eyes monstrous and it pounces or say some badass one liner. Other splats may have it a bit less.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-22T03:54:42-07:00","id":1563333,"title":"","content":"Okay, this is off-topic, but I want to get it off my chest.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563102-i-ve-come-to-really-appreciate-the-storyteller-v20-or-earlier-wound-system?p=1563114#post1563114\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">D&amp;D isn't perfect, but it gives one important option: to treat HP as luck. You never got hit in the first place. As an action hero you can push through a lot of stuff, but you also just get to brush with death and get away with it time and again. Part fate, part the luck of the daring, and part confidence that you can still push further.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an explanation of D&amp;D health that I firmly disagree with.<br /> <br /> It's both abstract, and flat out inconsistent with the internal fiction as a holistic matter. If D&amp;D health is &quot;Luck&quot;, what exactly are healing spells and potions doing? Why are there different damage types, if HP only tracks the failure to be hit? If you take poison damage, doesn't that require you to have poison in your veins? If you take fire damage, doesn't that mean you got burned? What about spells or effects where you're set on fire (yes, they do exist), doesn't that mean you're ON FIRE, being burned the whole way? If armor is meant to stop damage and it gets bypassed, are you supposed to be DOUBLE not injured (assuming HP is just &quot;Luck&quot;)?<br /> <br /> Reducing hit points to just &quot;how long you avoid not getting hit&quot; sounds like a reasonable explanation...until it's put into the game's wider context. At which point, &quot;HP-as-Luck&quot; starts raising too many questions.<br /> <br /> I won't demand that you or anyone else divest of the &quot;HP-as-Luck&quot; concept, if that's what you need to make sense of health in the game. But everything AROUND hit points is very obviously written with the assumption that when a character loses hit points, it's actual injury inflicted to the creature. That is the internal fiction <i>intended by the designers</i>.<br /> <br /> Why are characters able to fight with no penalty until they hit 0 HP? The Doylist answer is that Arneson and Gygax considered it a sufficiently workable abstraction to get the game on with.<br /> <br /> The Watsonian explanation could be any number of things. Up to and including adrenaline keep a fighter in battle despite injury, resistance to shock (with PCs of higher levels becoming toughened against hardship and pain), or anything else. You're not meant to think that hard about it. But it's obvious you ARE meant to assume when your PC gets hit with a battleaxe and take 1d8 + whatever Slashing damage (1d10 if two-handed), your PC was actually hurt. If that drops them to 0 HP, it's because that was more than they could take and they passed out (and are now dying).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-22T08:59:29-07:00","id":1563341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563102-i-ve-come-to-really-appreciate-the-storyteller-v20-or-earlier-wound-system?p=1563333#post1563333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, this is off-topic, but I want to get it off my chest.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This is an explanation of D&amp;D health that I firmly disagree with.</div> </div> </div> </div>And you're 100% wrong.<br /> <br /> Not because those aren't valid considerations, but mainly because none of those problems get solved.<br /> <br /> On damage types, for one: in every edition ever damage type only matters at the moment of taking damage. Past this point, it's just damage and no, you're not required to bear any injury.<br /> <br /> You are right that many effects assume some injury happened, and many others can't be just brushed off. Poison type damage is irrelevant, but it usually is accompanied by some poison effect (poisoned condition in 5th E).<br /> <br /> But the fiction also regularly assumes the PCs have perfectly normal anatomy and shouldn't by any stretch of the imagination be hit in the face by a giant scythe or engulfed by dragon's fire and live to tell the story. Massive damage rules and Coup de Grace also existed in one form or another for every edition. They only work if HP is <b>not</b> actual health, and actual hits do a lot more.<br /> <br /> Some effects also require you to watch if you hit half your HP or similar threshold, and those effects almost always assume this is the point when you have your first actual bleeding injury. It is even called Bloodied in 4th E, and in some optional rules since AD&amp;D 2nd E.<br /> <br /> For every situation where any approach is best, there is another where it fails. The designers never had a single option consistently in mind and in several books through the years they said so directly, which adds another problem: the assumption that this is game as intended by the designers is refuted <i>by the designers</i>.<br /> <br /> Whatever you run HP as, be it actual physical integrity, luck, confidence, a mix of options, it will remain a functional yet flawed abstraction. Healing effects are recovering your health, or your confidence or your luck in a measurable manner. Damage types work because by type of damage you have a closer or farther brush with death. Armor and shields guarantee you're neither hit or feel threatened. We don't track on how much kinetic energy bypasses the armor or how much is it chipped by attacks, after all.<br /> <br /> Even if designers wanted to model such things would just be stupid, because you can't really capture in simple rules how physical integrity really works. Whenever you think a system gets close enough even if with some stretching, it is more probable that you don't understand the dynamics of an injury. The system in the end is abstracting and making arbitrary calls whether the dev notices this or not. Some are just more self-aware of this, and I prefer those. I don't like systems who try to simulate reality because I understand reality enough to lose suspension of disbelief with them at some point, inevitably.<br /> <br /> Just to be clear: despite the reactive first line, I have nothing against whom likes to describe it as real injury. I think the fun of it is in being able to do whatever floats your boat, and since no take solves all inconsistencies, it's perfectly valid in every way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-23T14:27:35-07:00","id":1563377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1563102-i-ve-come-to-really-appreciate-the-storyteller-v20-or-earlier-wound-system?p=1563333#post1563333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, this is off-topic, but I want to get it off my chest.<br /> <br /> This is an explanation of D&amp;D health that I firmly disagree with.<br /> <br /> It's both abstract, and flat out inconsistent with the internal fiction as a holistic matter. If D&amp;D health is &quot;Luck&quot;, what exactly are healing spells and potions doing? <br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>The take by Gygax and probably some others is that it's a combination of physical damage and other things.<br /> <br /> So HP is both actual cuts, bruises, dented armor, lacerations etcetera, but also exhaustion(physical and mental), increased lack of focus, pain disrupting your stance, possible some minor damage to armor or weapons and so on. Not sure about 'luck' but you could probably throw that in there.<br /> <br /> In that perspective healing effects both heals physical damage and reinvigorates. Clears the head, restores stamina, let's you breathe easier. <br /> <br /> How much it's one or the other depends on how fantastical you want the heroes to be and is up to the gm.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure if I read Gygax take on it on dragonsfoot or in some white dwarf magazine but I faintly remember him talking about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}